From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Date: Sat, 23 Jul 1994 23:06:34 -0400
Subject: [#94-7-203] GS: On-line translation?

    I see one big problem with all artificial languages - there are no
(or too few) speakers or writers.  This seems to turn new language
projects into academic exercises with little practical consequences.
 
    Now what if we use translation software that would show us the text
the way we want it?
 
    Things like customized spelling would be trivial.  Also,
relatively simple conversions can let you see any text with your
favorite date formats (and calendar), convert between decimal and
other number systems, use metric measures if you are advanced enough
to understand them, implement obscenity filters, custom abbreviations,
synonim selections (I may want to read "dolls for boys" as more clear
and GS-correct, rather than "action figures"), etc., etc. 
 
    Translating English to E', let alone something more distant,
can be difficult, but still may take less effort than translation
between English and, say, German, as only a part of semantics has
to be processed.
 
    If the translations still appear difficult, maybe writing the
base texts in some "GS-base" language would ease the things.
 
  I keep reading about advances in such areas;
It seems that many of such features can be easily implemented now;
 
  As for the consequences, such translation agents may allow much
faster linguistic and cultural evolution and diversification.
In fact, I can hardly think of a greater historical opportunity
for rapid linguistic diversification than appearance of intelligent
software agents. 



