From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Sun, 24 Jul 94 13:17:43 -0700
Subject: [#94-7-204] FAQ: a bottom-up approach

I've started work on yet another incarnation of the FAQ, using a new
approach that I think will yield something that will be useful even in
partial form. My idea is to list the actual questions that I have seen
asked on the list (and answered!) several times, the sort of questions
that most often end up provoking long, drawn-out, and largely
pointless debates about "The Basics".

Currently, I've written answers for two questions: "Why do so many
extropians oppose the government on nearly every issue?" and "Why do
so many extropians oppose environmentalism?"

I'd like to ask for other candidates for questions that address the
basics, that have been asked often enough on the list to have
detracted from its quality for some of you, and that you think belong
in the FAQ. Also, if any of you object to the way I've chosen to word
the above two questions, I'd like to get your suggestions for better
wording (which might requires changes in the answers I've written).
After I've allowed enough time for some of this immediate feedback,
I'll post the first draft of the two-question proto-FAQ, and those of
you who want to can start tearing it to pieces. And perhaps I'll write
some more answers to the other frequently-asked questions you suggest.

My previous effort in this direction, namely my world-wide-web pages
at http:/www.c2.org/~arkuat/extropy.html, is still available for
anyone who wants to browse it. But Hypertext Markup Language is not
exactly the sort of medium we can post to alt.extropians, or email to
new list members.

I *know* that FAQ efforts have a long history of flickering out after
meager beginnings. That's why I'm designing this document so that even
partial, incomplete incarnations will be useful... so that we can get
80% of the value from the first 20% of the effort. Starting out with a
big ambitious outline and then filling in the content lacks this
virtue, though that approach has other virtues of its own. I hope that
what my effort produces will be easy to incorporate into the "large,
complete outline" approach in the future.

Eric Watt Forste || finger arkuat@c2.org || http://www.c2.org/~arkuat



