From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Mon, 25 Jul 1994 01:41:00 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-7-207] FAQ & medical ethics

Eric Watt Forste:
> My idea is to list the actual questions that I have seen
> asked on the list (and answered!) several times, the sort of questions
> that most often end up provoking long, drawn-out, and largely
> pointless debates about "The Basics".

A very good idea!  You'd think it would be an obvious idea, 
given that the acronym stands for Frequently Asked Questions, 
but it seems to have been forgotten these days.  I've seen
several "FAQ" projects started even before any questions have 
been asked!

A web site is also a splendid medium.  This way we can provide
convenient links to the cryonics, life extension, nanotechnology, 
cypherpunks, libertarian, etc. FAQs and "best of" article sites 
instead of duplicating their work. 

Here's a question -- not necessarily frequent, but perhaps one
of our most important areas of concern that isn't already
covered by other organizations or discussion forums.  How far 
along are we in developing an Extropian medical ethics?  Many, 
perhaps most of the barriers to cryonics, smart drugs[1,2], 
and other medical enhancements are ethical in nature.
So many people in our society consider these activities 
unethical that they are strongly discouraged in many ways:
harassment by professional societies, lack of funding
for education and research, import controls, regulations, etc.
All of the arguments of "medical ethicists" I've seen quoted
have been anti-enhancement.  We need to develop a corpus
of pro-enhancement arguments, including arguments orthogonal
to the free-market argument.  (ie arguments that also apply
to government-run health care systems).

[1] cf. excellent article in the Wednesday, July 20th Wall
Street Journal, page B1: "Memory Drug Appears to Aid Rodent
Recall".  Ampakine(tm) appears to double recall on a test
of 8-hour memory in healthy rats.  The article discusses how 
such drugs, if they are useful for the targeted "ethical" market
(restoring memory in Alzheimer's patients) would probably
also be useful for enhancing memory in healthy people.
Doubling recall would dwarf the effects of current "smart
drugs".  Such enhancement could be much larger market than
Alzheimer's threatment, but the drug developers are discounting 
the enhancement market due to "social and regulatory concerns".  
Much more money would be spent to develop smart drugs if 
memory enhancement were considered ethically acceptable.

[2] cf. also the excellent book _Listening to Prozac_ by
Peter D. Kramer, and the rebutall _Talking Back to Prozac_.
These are probably the two most accessible sources for the
current state of debate over mental enhancement.  The fact
that Prozac can be used for enhancement as well as treatment
of illness is considered by many a strong ethical argument
against using Prozac even to treat illness!

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

